{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.23e8c7ba31","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-ne-86","passage":"<p>The orchestra's youngest member, a thirteen-year-old ______ a solo at the gala.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["cellist—performed","cellist; performed","cellist, performed","cellist performed"],"answer":"C","explanation":"The phrase “a thirteen-year-old cellist” is extra information about “the orchestra's youngest member.” It opens with a comma, so it must close with the same punctuation: a pair of <b>commas</b>. Mixing punctuation marks or leaving the element unclosed is incorrect."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.dc3a9e46e6","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-3","passage":"<p>The coach reminded the players of the team's two ______ and communication.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["priorities; defense","priorities, and defense","priorities: defense","priorities defense"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The coach reminded the players of the team's two priorities” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.0f2e23db60","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-15","passage":"<p>The chef follows one rule in her ______ use of fresh, local ingredients whenever possible.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["kitchen: the","kitchen; the","kitchen, and the","kitchen the"],"answer":"A","explanation":"“The chef follows one rule in her kitchen” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.4ace1419d2","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-28","passage":"<p>The kit includes everything a beginner ______, paints, a palette, and a small canvas.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["needs; brushes","needs, brushes","needs brushes","needs: brushes"],"answer":"D","explanation":"“The kit includes everything a beginner needs” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a list that specifies it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces such a list. A semicolon must join two independent clauses, and a comma or no punctuation fails to set off the list clearly."},
{"id":"g.rw.boundaries.2.ca42bfb0e7","section":"rw","domain":"conventions","skill":"boundaries","difficulty":2,"group":"bnd-colon-37","passage":"<p>The scientist's research focuses on a single ______ plants sense changes in temperature.</p>","prompt":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["question how","question, and how","question: how","question; how"],"answer":"C","explanation":"“The scientist's research focuses on a single question” is a complete independent clause, and what follows is a phrase that explains or identifies something in it. A <b>colon</b> correctly introduces that explanation. A semicolon needs an independent clause on both sides, and “and” wrongly joins a clause to a phrase."}
